  │ Delivery   │ In-app adapter only, written against the channel interface.                                         │
  ├────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ UI         │ Task list, task edit, bell with unread count, dropdown list, mark-read. 30s poll.                   │
  └────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  [1mExplicitly deferred[0m — named here so they're decisions, not oversights: explicit watch/mute (seam exists), email
  channel (interface exists), [38;5;153mtask.commented[39m and [38;5;153mtask.due_date_changed[39m, per-type preferences, SSE/WebSocket, permissions
  and task visibility, e2e and property tests.

  [1mTesting.[0m Unit tests, written first (TDD). The valuable ones:

  - [38;5;153minterest.ts[39m — table-driven: actor excluded; creator and assignee both notified; creator-is-assignee yields one
    recipient, not two; unassigned task notifies only the creator.
  - [38;5;153mcoalesce.ts[39m — pure function, so this is where the edge cases get pinned: same key merges inside the window,
    different type doesn't merge, closed window starts a new notification, an open window is [1mnot[0m extended by new events.
  - Worker — process the same event twice, assert exactly one notification exists (this is the test that proves the
    [38;5;153mUNIQUE(event_id, recipient_id)[39m index does its job). A poison event doesn't block the ones behind it.
  - Dispatch — a failing adapter marks its delivery failed without affecting other channels.
  - HTTP — [38;5;153mGET /notifications[39m returns only the session user's rows. This is the security test; it should exist from the
    first commit.

  The two [38;5;153mcoalesce.ts[39m cases about windows, and the worker idempotency case, are the ones I'd expect to actually catch
  bugs. Everything else is regression insurance.
